<Project>

  <!-- The two numbers a person decides. Nothing else lives in this file, and that is the point: the
       build number below them is counted from the commits made since this file last changed, so an
       edit here - and only an edit here - starts the count again. Keeping them in Directory.Build.props
       meant that turning on warnings-as-errors reset the build number, which is not a version bump by
       any reading.

       OrbitMajorVersion - the move to production. Orbit runs on a test environment today, publicly
       reachable but still a test one; the day it moves to its own address this becomes 1. Nothing else
       raises it.

       OrbitPatchVersion - a milestone, raised by hand when somebody decides one has been reached. It is
       the number that says a release is a different thing rather than the next of the same, and it is
       deliberately not derivable from anything: "far enough to matter" is a judgement, not a count. -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <OrbitMajorVersion>0</OrbitMajorVersion>
    <OrbitPatchVersion>3</OrbitPatchVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
